{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-07_16-54-34-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run/draft && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfb={\\\"ref_ids\\\":[\\\"a0f10ee024ac4ba38d3b84b21a8b40f3\\\",\\\"32aff0d098454dc78bec6a3916753fa9\\\",\\\"05dc6a3632954371911663108e7e18e8\\\",\\\"52a176deb66c4588a59d71f8afeb13ab\\\",\\\"B5-slow-path-accept\\\",\\\"F2-committed-decision\\\",\\\"S20-exec\\\",\\\"S15-handlePrepareReply\\\"],\n \\\"answered\\\":\\\"Two things. First, the C1 agreement finding is confirmed again on the amended claim: the fresh execution bound to claim version 2 (artifact a0f10ee024ac4ba38d3b84b21a8b40f3, run 32aff0d098454dc78bec6a3916753fa9) reproduced the three in-scope observations and the current-version review accepted their correspondence, so the conclusion is confirmed_in_scope with the pre-amendment artifacts superseded for basis rather than used. Second, the remaining open issue ef6b54abce294555b31a3c9a6ca606ac is structurally unresolvable within the mechanisms this framework accepts: resolving it on the issue's own artifact is refused because a scope-challenged issue needs a new artifact with the challenged component changed and a matching fresh execution (the artifact's bytes cannot change without an oracle or observation challenge); resolving it on the fresh artifact is refused because that artifact is not in the issue's lineage; an ordinary repair is refused because it must preserve the oracle, which dropping the applied-state property does not; and an encoding revision is refused because the challenge is about scope rather than the oracle or the observation. The issue therefore documents a boundary that is already recorded in the claim, C3 and the reviews, and depends on no substantive disagreement.\\\",\n \\\"remaining\\\":[\\\"the issue stays visible as an attribution note on a superseded artifact; nothing in the findings depends on it\\\",\n   \\\"the reader-side consequence of the codec shift (what a replica does once its stream is displaced) is unread\\\",\n   \\\"whether the client library caps the command size it builds, and whether any caller enables durable recording, are unread\\\",\n   \\\"the two paused candidates keep their resume conditions: the accept-status question and the client-retry question\\\"],\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Recording the dead end prevents further turns from repeating four refused mechanisms, and recording the confirmed re-execution keeps the run's substantive state unambiguous. The lesson for the process is concrete: amending a claim's scope invalidates its artifacts' bases, so an artifact challenged on scope should be revised in the same cycle as the challenge, or the issue accepted as permanently visible.\\\",\n \\\"understanding\\\":\\\"updated\\\",\\\"question_updates\\\":{}}\nsub={\\\"action\\\":\\\"research\\\",\\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Final bookkeeping note: the C1 findings are confirmed again on the amended claim version, and the single open issue is shown to be structurally unresolvable through the mechanisms the validator accepts, with each refusal quoted. No map object changes and no requirement, premise or observation is affected.\\\",\n \\\"feedback\\\":fb}\njson.dump(sub,open('submission-final-note.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)\nprint('ok')\nPY\ncd .. && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-07_16-54-34-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run --submission submission-final-note.json 2>&1 | head -8\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
